1
00:00:01,260 --> 00:00:03,400
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:04,080
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:09,580
My name is Catherine and today we have the beginning of a very long series on giving a presentation.

4
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:12,720
So this series is at the intermediate level, right Marco?

5
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:12,920
That's right.

6
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:14,820
It's part of our business series.

7
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:18,060
We previously took a look at how to go upon an interview.

8
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:20,760
Now we're going to talk about presentation.

9
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:27,340
So everything about a presentation, how to give a presentation, language you can use in a presentation and a lot of other things like this.

10
00:00:27,850 --> 00:00:28,270
That's right.

11
00:00:28,750 --> 00:00:31,070
We're going to talk about the very beginning of a presentation.

12
00:00:31,510 --> 00:00:34,090
Let's take a listen to this dialogue and we'll be back in a moment.

13
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:39,860
Hi everyone.

14
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:41,020
Can everyone hear me?

15
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:43,940
Can you guys at the back hear everything?

16
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:50,450
I think all of you know why we are here this afternoon.

17
00:00:51,260 --> 00:00:55,050
As most of you are aware, 2010 marks an important moment for Alpha Computers.

18
00:00:55,720 --> 00:01:00,570
We have bounced back from the recession and now we are set to launch our new line of laptop and desktop computers.

19
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:11,850
I'm really pleased to welcome Michael Ford, the global marketing manager for Alpha Computers, who has flown in from California to give all of you an overview of the marketing campaign and to answer any questions you may have.

20
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:14,310
So please, give a warm welcome to Mr. Ford.

21
00:01:17,030 --> 00:01:17,810
Thank you, Jonathan.

22
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:20,780
It really is a pleasure to be here today.

23
00:01:21,520 --> 00:01:27,460
It has been three years since I visited Beijing and it's clear to me that operations here are obviously going from strength to strength.

24
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:33,860
The Alpha brand continues to grow in leaps and bounds in China and that is certainly down to the hard work of all of you here.

25
00:01:34,540 --> 00:01:36,140
So congratulations to all of you.

26
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:46,920
I'd like to start by outlining the key points of my presentation this afternoon and giving you an idea of the topics that will be discussed.

27
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:50,360
The presentation today is divided into five main parts.

28
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:59,930
First of all, I'd like to briefly touch on the background of the new X420 line, how the whole concept has come about and how the new product fits into our existing brand line.

29
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:03,870
Secondly, I'd like to present data on projected sales for the X420.

30
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:07,870
We will then go on to discuss our key rivals in this sector.

31
00:02:08,480 --> 00:02:11,270
Then I'd like to go on to outline the campaign concept for the X420.

32
00:02:12,110 --> 00:02:16,730
Finally, I'm happy to open up the discussion for any questions or points you may have for me.

33
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:22,560
All right, we're back.

34
00:02:23,080 --> 00:02:25,340
So as you can see, it's the very beginning of a presentation.

35
00:02:25,880 --> 00:02:29,760
We're kind of starting to give people an idea what we're going to talk about.

36
00:02:29,980 --> 00:02:34,040
So let's take a look at some of the language Mr. Ford used on Language Takeaway.

37
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:39,600
Language Takeaway.

38
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:46,140
So the reason Mr. Ford is giving a presentation is because he wants to talk about the company's new line of computers, right?

39
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:52,600
And so he says that they're going to launch a new line of desktop and laptop computers.

40
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:52,800
That's right.

41
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:56,160
So they want to launch a new line of laptops.

42
00:02:56,160 --> 00:03:00,280
So this verb to launch means to bring out to the market.

43
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:01,080
That's right.

44
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:08,270
So instead of the word to start, which you could use in many different ways, like to start eating or to start swimming.

45
00:03:09,310 --> 00:03:13,630
In this case, to launch means you're going to introduce a new product to the market.

46
00:03:13,790 --> 00:03:14,330
Like you said, Marco.

47
00:03:14,670 --> 00:03:15,130
That's right.

48
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,280
So we're going to start selling to the market a new product.

49
00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:22,370
We're going to launch a new line of laptop and desktop computers.

50
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:28,490
For example, this spring, Steve Jobs launched the iPad, which is a new product.

51
00:03:29,160 --> 00:03:29,350
That's right.

52
00:03:30,160 --> 00:03:32,050
So he launched the iPad and the iPhone 4.

53
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:35,250
They're always launching new things at Apple.

54
00:03:35,720 --> 00:03:35,970
That's right.

55
00:03:36,720 --> 00:03:36,950
All right.

56
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:46,230
So since they're launching this new line of laptop and desktop computers, Mr. Ford first wanted to give a brief overview of the marketing campaign.

57
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:47,720
Okay.

58
00:03:47,720 --> 00:03:51,890
So first of all, we're going to talk about how this product is being marketed.

59
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:57,920
So you want to give a general picture or a very simple picture of what's going to happen.

60
00:03:57,920 --> 00:03:59,510
So at the beginning, this will happen.

61
00:03:59,830 --> 00:04:01,090
Then this will happen at the end.

62
00:04:01,110 --> 00:04:01,690
This will happen.

63
00:04:01,830 --> 00:04:02,970
And that's what we call an overview.

64
00:04:03,710 --> 00:04:04,050
That's right.

65
00:04:04,370 --> 00:04:09,450
So it's a brief or general idea of what we're talking about.

66
00:04:09,630 --> 00:04:11,110
An overview, nothing too specific.

67
00:04:11,210 --> 00:04:12,790
We just want to give you a general idea.

68
00:04:13,170 --> 00:04:13,490
That's right.

69
00:04:14,110 --> 00:04:22,990
After that, Mr. Ford starts talking and he's talking about the presentation, which has many parts, and he wants to talk about what each part will contain.

70
00:04:23,330 --> 00:04:27,870
So he says, I'd like to briefly touch on the background of the new X420 line.

71
00:04:28,070 --> 00:04:29,250
So this verb, to touch on.

72
00:04:29,550 --> 00:04:30,070
What does this mean?

73
00:04:30,350 --> 00:04:30,510
Okay.

74
00:04:31,760 --> 00:04:37,680
So when he says he wants to touch on the background of this new computer, he's basically saying, I want to talk about.

75
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:38,680
All right.

76
00:04:38,680 --> 00:04:44,610
So this is potentially very confusing because we know the word to touch means to put your hand on something.

77
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:49,850
But to touch on, this is a phrasal verb, this means to talk about or to address.

78
00:04:50,440 --> 00:04:51,440
That's right.

79
00:04:51,440 --> 00:04:57,110
So for example, a teacher at a university will touch on many topics during his lecture.

80
00:04:57,840 --> 00:04:57,990
That's right.

81
00:04:58,840 --> 00:05:01,650
Or I'd like to touch on where we're going to dinner tonight because I'm very hungry.

82
00:05:02,120 --> 00:05:03,120
Okay.

83
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:05,970
So basically it's a nice way of saying, I want to talk about this topic.

84
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:15,490
And then apart from touching on the background of the new computer, he said he also wants to talk about projected sales for this new computer.

85
00:05:16,050 --> 00:05:16,210
Okay.

86
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:19,310
So projected sales, this phrase includes two words.

87
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:26,130
Sales means how much of something you sell, obviously, but projected means what you expect to sell.

88
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:29,450
So the projected sales have not happened yet.

89
00:05:29,770 --> 00:05:30,950
They will happen in the future.

90
00:05:30,950 --> 00:05:33,870
And so they're basically my guess about sales.

91
00:05:34,350 --> 00:05:34,890
That's right.

92
00:05:35,410 --> 00:05:39,290
So basically we are giving an estimate of what we think we will sell.

93
00:05:39,550 --> 00:05:40,810
Those are projected sales.

94
00:05:41,120 --> 00:05:43,520
We think we will sell a thousand computers.

95
00:05:43,520 --> 00:05:46,120
Those are our projected sales.

96
00:05:46,120 --> 00:05:47,120
Right.

97
00:05:47,120 --> 00:05:48,630
And after that, we have one more key word.

98
00:05:48,920 --> 00:05:51,430
This word comes at the very end of the speech.

99
00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:56,110
Mr. Ford says, we will then go on to discuss our key rivals.

100
00:05:56,600 --> 00:05:57,890
Now who is a rival, Marco?

101
00:05:58,050 --> 00:05:58,350
Okay.

102
00:05:58,690 --> 00:06:03,410
So a rival is basically your competitor or maybe kind of your enemy.

103
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:04,480
All right.

104
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:06,600
So in computers, who is Apple's rival?

105
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:10,760
Maybe HP or IBM or Dell.

106
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:11,940
Very good.

107
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:15,800
So a rival is someone you compete with.

108
00:06:15,800 --> 00:06:15,960
That's right.

109
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:18,480
So he's going to talk about his key rivals.

110
00:06:18,880 --> 00:06:22,500
So basically the word key is your most important rivals.

111
00:06:23,100 --> 00:06:23,660
All right.

112
00:06:23,660 --> 00:06:27,360
So for example, who is one of Batman's key rivals?

113
00:06:27,980 --> 00:06:30,520
Because we have rivals not just in business, but also in life.

114
00:06:31,160 --> 00:06:31,380
That's right.

115
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:34,500
So I think maybe Batman's key rival might be the Joker.

116
00:06:35,160 --> 00:06:35,780
Or the Penguin.

117
00:06:36,160 --> 00:06:37,160
Or the Penguin.

118
00:06:37,160 --> 00:06:38,160
All right.

119
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:41,720
So let's take another listen to today's dialogue and we'll be back in a moment with some very important phrases.

120
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:47,660
Hi, everyone.

121
00:06:48,020 --> 00:06:48,840
Can everyone hear me?

122
00:06:50,170 --> 00:06:51,730
Can you guys at the back hear everything?

123
00:06:55,850 --> 00:06:58,350
I think all of you know why we are here this afternoon.

124
00:06:59,050 --> 00:07:02,950
As most of you are aware, 2010 marks an important moment for Alpha Computers.

125
00:07:03,390 --> 00:07:08,510
